  "id": "Compound::DB00679 - Gene::150",
  "verbalization": "Thioridazine is implicated in the regulation of Adra2a through a series of interconnected pathways involving antipsychotic agents and their binding to adrenergic receptors. The evidence suggests that Thioridazine, as an antipsychotic agent, causes choreoathetoid movements, which are subsequently mediated by fluphenazine or loxapine, both of which bind to Adra2a. This indicates a potential mechanism where Thioridazine exerts its effects via the activation of adrenergic receptors, possibly through the indirect action of its metabolites or via a shared pathway with other antipsychotics. The lowest common ancestors of Thioridazine and its target agents point to a common class of compounds, reinforcing the notion of a broader pharmacological relationship. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Adra2a in the central nervous system, suggesting that Thioridazine may influence adrenergic signaling through its antipsychotic properties.",
